#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084381} :activity/id #uuid "44d765ee-f9a3-51fd-a9fb-673e5f0fd77d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050235 :topic/id #uuid "3816ddcf-eb6f-59e3-80aa-5b965a06b098" :topic/math-academy-id 766} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084381 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084509 :tutorial/content "Let $\\triangle{ABC}$ be a triangle where the lengths $AB=7$ and $AC=5$ are fixed. For example, consider the triangle shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4c1df256d9a40c50e3535f3055fef041cf02710c0a9e0414acae1bf38681ff4.png)\n\n      \n\nNow, suppose that we vary the angle $\\angle{A}$, making it either very small or very large. What would be the maximum possible length of the side $\\overline {BC}$ for which the figure is still a triangle?  \n\nWe make the longest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by increasing the angle $\\angle{A}$ as shown in the sketches below. Here, we fix vertices $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ until $\\angle{A}$ measures $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/d9/d9777d021ad9aac118452b691567d325939c5c8591b777f2cf9c17b39916e660.png)\n\n          \n\nFrom the picture, we can see that when $m\\angle{A}=180^\\circ ,$ we will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be $7+5=12.$ Therefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be shorter than the sum of the other sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & <AB+AC \\\\  & <7+5 \\\\  & <12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is true for all triangles:\n\nIn every triangle, the length of any one side must be smaller than the sum of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is one part of the **triangle inequality theorem**, which we will continue building upon in this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "29fc4755-2259-5126-bad7-5e7c73c355b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8192 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "13ee6ddf-a2c8-5588-8e52-f8a84af5eba4" :step/math-academy-id 73696 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084382}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084382 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083395 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082921 :question/id #uuid "25a73406-c869-5786-af97-cf90866eb1a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "768204cd-ee1f-5271-9ec9-71d672b4eb9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048336}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497387}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Impossible Sides of a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "a2043004-3f75-55f7-8ceb-e7960310878d" :step/math-academy-id 73697 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084383}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084383 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084510 :tutorial/content "Consider the same triangle as before, with sides $5$ and $7.$ This time, what is the shortest possible length of the side $\\overline {BC},$ for which the figure is still a triangle?\n\nWe make the shortest possible segment $\\overline{BC}$ by decreasing the angle $\\angle{A}.$ Again, we fix $A$ and $B$, and then \"rotate\" the side $\\overline{AC}$ about the vertex $A$ towards the side $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d1bd3363c2c218c6a2813a957bace3d29b7240919ced63d58be299e9478c099.png)\n\n         \n\nFrom the picture, we can see that for $m\\angle{A}=0^\\circ$ we will not have a triangle anymore and the length of $\\overline{BC}$ will be $7-5=2.$ Therefore, if we would like to have a triangle, $\\overline{BC}$ must be longer than the difference of the other sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & >AB-AC \\\\  & >7-5 \\\\  & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is true for all triangles: \n\nIn every triangle, the length of any one side must be larger than the difference of the other two sides.\n\nThe above statement is the other part of the **triangle inequality theorem**." :tutorial/id #uuid "64f2a60c-c156-5226-a994-9c2f141a59db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8193 :tutorial/title "The Difference of Two Sides"} :step/id #uuid "a2714886-a7e1-52ee-ba1b-b1922a69c5ca" :step/math-academy-id 73700 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084384}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084384 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084511 :tutorial/content "All of the discussion so far can be summarized in the following statement that is called the **triangle inequality theorem**.\n\n*In a triangle, the length of a side must be greater than the difference of the lengths of the other two sides and less than the sum of those lengths.*\n\nLet's write that down using mathematical notations. Suppose that in a triangle the length of a side equals $a$ and the other two sides have length $b$ and $c$ with $b>c.$ Then, we must have \n\n$$\nb - c < a < b + c.\n$$\n\nIf we would like to omit the assumption that $b>c$, we can still use the same inequality, as long as we include an absolute value on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n| b - c |  < a < b + c\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cd8d2616-ebae-5d43-89d3-470c89118c36" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8194 :tutorial/title "The Triangle Inequality Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "85378e79-6c45-5c8a-9009-8d28559db885" :step/math-academy-id 73701 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084385}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083396 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082922 :question/id #uuid "fa9395a9-f5b8-5c80-87e0-49da33706994" :question/math-academy-id "e-13102"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f668f90-7140-5471-8638-18a38eae5e07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497408}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Possible Lengths for a Side of a Triangle Given the Other Sides"} :step/id #uuid "abd1e9c3-f22d-5627-bb7d-521c942987a6" :step/math-academy-id 73702 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082923 :question/id #uuid "dddb137b-afbb-515c-b44a-17b9ab2d667e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13103"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8e827c3-95a0-5270-8323-f2512b64edd0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497397}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum or Minimum Length of a Side of a Triangle: Word Problems"} :step/id #uuid "381f23e4-4ced-5b30-8ea4-c24b78bb3ff1" :step/math-academy-id 73705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082924 :question/id #uuid "1512acb2-ac78-557a-b6a7-5b37addbeb24" :question/math-academy-id "e-13104"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3736cf03-5a3a-5a22-8338-63cdabb1c046" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497376}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Upper Bound for a Length"} :step/id #uuid "1cc41227-f39d-5f7a-a0e4-998e7c74302e" :step/math-academy-id 73708}] :activity/title "The Triangle Inequality" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083455}]}
